E = [
("02-01","Erasmus Hall in Flatbush produced two American icons","Brooklyn","1959","Barbra Streisand and Neil Diamond both sang in the chorus at Erasmus Hall High School on Flatbush Avenue, a short walk from Cortelyou Road. They barely knew each other then.",["They later recorded the duet 'You Don't Bring Me Flowers' in 1978","Clive Davis, who signed Springsteen and Whitney Houston, also went to Erasmus"],"crowd"),
("02-02","The Kings Theatre sat dark for 36 years","Brooklyn","1977","The Flatbush Avenue movie palace closed in 1977 and decayed with trees growing through the roof. A restoration reopened it in 2015 as one of Brooklyn's great music rooms.",["Original 1929 plasterwork was recast from surviving fragments","It now seats roughly 3,000 for concerts a mile from Ditmas Park"],"crowd"),
("02-03","Biggie recorded his debut while still selling on the corner","Hip-Hop","1994","'Ready to Die' was cut while Christopher Wallace was living on St. James Place in Clinton Hill. Producers have described him writing nothing down, holding entire verses in his head.",["He never used a pen in the booth, a habit Jay-Z also adopted","The album's skits map his actual Brooklyn upbringing"],"mic"),
("02-04","The Beastie Boys started as a Brooklyn hardcore band","Punk","1981","Before rapping, the Beastie Boys played fast hardcore punk around New York. Their pivot to hip-hop began as a joke track that got better reactions than their punk set.",["Their first release was a hardcore EP, not a rap record","They kept playing instruments themselves long after going platinum"],"guitar"),
("02-05","Coney Island's boardwalk had its own sound","Brooklyn","1950","Amusement park organs, barkers and competing radios created a dense collage that New York songwriters mined for decades. The Tilyou family's Steeplechase organ was audible for blocks.",["Band organs were built to cut through crowd noise without amplification","Brighton Beach's Russian clubs later added a second musical layer nearby"],"crowd"),
("02-06","Bob Marley was born on this day","Reggae","1945","Born in rural Nine Mile, Jamaica, Marley moved to the Kingston slum of Trench Town as a teenager, where the Wailers formed around vocal-group harmony, not roots reggae.",["Their earliest records were ska, much faster than what he became known for","He studied welding before committing to music"],"mic"),
("02-07","Brooklyn's Fort Greene birthed a jazz-rap scene","Hip-Hop","1990","In the early nineties, Fort Greene brownstones hosted a circle of artists that fed groups like Digable Planets and later Black Star's Mos Def and Talib Kweli.",["Kweli co-owned Nkiru Books, a Park Slope Black bookstore","Black Star's debut leaned on live jazz samples rather than hard drums"],"turntable"),
("02-08","The click track was invented to sync film, not music","Film","1930","Early sound-film composers punched holes in the film itself to make audible clicks, letting orchestras lock to the picture. Recording studios borrowed it decades later.",["Musicians initially resisted it as mechanical","Today nearly every pop record is built on a grid descended from it"],"tape"),
("02-09","The Beatles played Ed Sullivan to 73 million people","Rock","1964","Broadcast from CBS Studio 50 in Manhattan, the performance was watched by roughly 40 percent of America. Crime rates that hour reportedly dipped.",["The studio received 50,000 ticket requests for 700 seats","Sullivan had booked them after seeing fan chaos at Heathrow"],"radio"),
("02-10","Dub was born by accident when a vocal track was left off","Reggae","1968","Engineer King Tubby pressed a version of a record with the vocals missing. Dancers loved it, so he began deliberately stripping tracks and drenching them in echo.",["He rewired mixing desks by hand to create his effects","Dub's remix logic is the direct ancestor of electronic dance music"],"mixer"),
("02-11","Whitney Houston's mother sang backup for Aretha","Soul","1967","Cissy Houston led the Sweet Inspirations, who sang behind Aretha Franklin and Elvis. Whitney grew up in studios watching that work.",["Dionne Warwick is her cousin","Cissy also sang on Van Morrison's 'Brown Eyed Girl' sessions era material"],"mic"),
("02-12","Handel wrote 'Messiah' in 24 days","Classical","1741","Working almost without sleep, Handel completed the 259-page score in just over three weeks. A servant reported finding him weeping at his desk.",["It premiered in Dublin, not London","Audiences were asked to leave hoops and swords at home to fit more people in"],"strings"),
("02-13","The 'Loud War' shrank music's dynamic range","Production","1995","Mastering engineers began compressing records to sound louder than competitors on radio and CD changers. The side effect was flattened peaks and listening fatigue.",["Some nineties reissues are quieter than their originals by design","Streaming loudness normalization has since reduced the incentive"],"mixer"),
("02-14","Ol' Dirty Bastard was born in Fort Greene","Hip-Hop","1968","Russell Jones grew up in Brooklyn and took his stage name from a kung-fu film, claiming there was 'no father to his style'. His off-key delivery was deliberate.",["He was cousins with RZA and GZA of Wu-Tang","His vocal takes were often recorded in one pass with no punch-ins"],"mic"),
("02-15","Nat King Cole was a pianist first","Jazz","1943","Cole led an influential piano trio for years before singing became his main draw. He only sang because a club patron demanded it, by his own account.",["His trio format dropped drums, unusual for the era","He was among the first Black artists to host a national TV show"],"piano"),
("02-16","Barclays Center changed which artists tour Brooklyn","Brooklyn","2012","Before 2012, most arena acts skipped Brooklyn entirely for Manhattan or the Meadowlands. The Atlantic Avenue arena reversed that overnight.",["Jay-Z played the opening eight-night run","It sits atop one of the busiest transit hubs in the borough"],"crowd"),
("02-17","Autotune was invented by an oil industry engineer","Pop","1997","Andy Hildebrand used seismic data math, designed to find oil underground, to detect and correct pitch. He built it as a subtle repair tool.",["Cher's 'Believe' used the extreme setting the inventor called a joke","Hildebrand said he never expected anyone to use it that way"],"synth"),
("02-18","Yoko Ono and John Lennon recorded a heartbeat","Art Rock","1969","Their experimental albums included a recording of their unborn child's heartbeat, followed by silence after the pregnancy was lost. Few records have been that raw.",["Critics dismissed the work at the time","It anticipated decades of sound-art practice"],"tape"),
("02-19","Smokey Robinson wrote the song Motown almost rejected","Soul","1965","'The Tracks of My Tears' came from a guitar figure Marv Tarplin played for weeks before lyrics existed. Robinson wrote to the guitar, not the other way around.",["Bob Dylan reportedly called Robinson America's greatest living poet","The guitar intro was kept exactly as Tarplin first improvised it"],"guitar"),
("02-20","Kurt Cobain was born, and later hid melodies in noise","Grunge","1967","Cobain's writing method paired sweet pop melody with deliberately abrasive tone, a tension he credited to loving both the Beatles and Black Flag.",["'Nevermind' was mixed to sound more polished than the band wanted","He wrote lyrics minutes before recording many vocals"],"guitar"),
("02-21","Nina Simone played Carnegie Hall, but not as she planned","Jazz","1963","When Simone finally reached Carnegie Hall, she noted bitterly that she arrived as a pop singer, not the classical concert pianist she trained to be.",["She opened sets with Bach preludes to make the point","Her civil-rights songs cost her radio play and income"],"piano"),
("02-22","Flatbush's Caribbean records shaped Brooklyn radio","Brooklyn","1970","Waves of Caribbean migration made Flatbush a hub for calypso, soca and reggae pressings. Local record shops imported Jamaican dubplates before mainstream stores carried them.",["Sound systems set up in backyards and church halls across the neighborhood","That circuit fed directly into New York hip-hop's early sound"],"radio"),
("02-23","George Frideric Handel went blind writing music","Classical","1751","Handel continued composing and performing after losing his sight, dictating revisions from memory. Audiences reported he still improvised organ solos flawlessly.",["He was buried in Westminster Abbey with 3,000 mourners","His late oratorios were largely written without being able to see the page"],"strings"),
("02-24","The Sugarhill Gang were not a real group","Hip-Hop","1979","Label owner Sylvia Robinson assembled three unknowns after hearing rap at a party. One was auditioned while working at a pizzeria.",["Big Bank Hank reportedly borrowed rhymes from Grandmaster Caz","Caz received no credit or royalties for lines used on the record"],"turntable"),
("02-25","George Harrison's sitar lesson changed rock","Rock","1966","Harrison studied under Ravi Shankar, who insisted he learn properly rather than use the instrument as decoration. Indian modes then filtered into Western pop.",["Shankar initially had no idea who the Beatles were","'Within You Without You' features no other Beatle"],"strings"),
("02-26","Fats Domino sold more records than anyone but Elvis","R&B","1955","The New Orleans pianist's rolling triplets defined early rock piano. He recorded almost everything at Cosimo Matassa's tiny J&M studio.",["The studio was in the back of an appliance shop","Its cramped room created the signature slapback sound"],"piano"),
("02-27","Brooklyn's Prospect Park bandshell started a free summer tradition","Brooklyn","1979","Celebrate Brooklyn began as a modest neighborhood series and grew into one of America's longest-running free outdoor festivals, minutes from Ditmas Park.",["It launched partly to bring people back into the park after a rough decade","Acts range from Afrobeat legends to local Brooklyn bands"],"crowd"),
("02-28","The Moog synthesizer needed a hit to be taken seriously","Electronic","1968","Wendy Carlos's 'Switched-On Bach' proved the Moog could make music, not just noise. It became one of the best-selling classical records ever.",["Each note had to be played and layered one at a time","The instrument was monophonic, so chords required many passes"],"synth"),
("02-29","Leap day gave Gioachino Rossini a rare birthday","Classical","1792","Born on 29 February, Rossini joked he only aged once every four years. He retired from opera at 37 and spent decades writing small pieces and cooking.",["He wrote 'The Barber of Seville' in about three weeks","He named several compositions after food"],"strings"),
]
